hat we look at the next big thing that  seems great to adults and assume that  it's going to be the same thing for kids  so if you assign them to watch a video  for example at home one night that is  maybe 10 minutes long by 30 seconds in  if they're not really interested they  just kind of zone out and they come to  school the next day really believing  that they have watched the video in some  schools where summit was implemented two  parents fought to overturn it parents  and students often just don't know what  they don't know about school they don't  know what it could be what opportunities  they might be missing dos meses para la  familia the subjects that we cover and  the electronic apps are science history  geography we do music Spanish math art  according to Clayton Christensen's  theory of disruptive innovation change  happens first in the segments of the  markets so insignificant that nobody is  really paying attention to them but  eventually they grow to overtake the  industry disrupting class gives home  schoolers as an example  Melissa Keegan is a mother of five in  rural Maryland and I'm not I'm not a  teacher I'm not trained to be a teacher  I don't think you need to have that in  order to home-school  if you have apps like these we're living  in this moment I think where we can  reshape the models at the heart of  Education and leverage these tools and  technologies are we on the eve of seeing  the end of the traditional classroom no  reason why why it really can't happen in  every classroom in a man  tomorrow or perhaps Kristensen and horn  are guilty as the saying goes of  mistaking a clear view of the future for  a short distance  the current classroom model may survive  for a long time to come  at least until software gets better at  approximating student-teacher  interactions and when change does occur  chances are it will look nothing like we  had imagined  [Music]
 

